{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.55,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_component_type",
      "components": ["T4"],
      "pins": ["T4.1", "T4.3", "T4.4"],
      "nets": ["/+24V_FB", "+24V"],
      "observed": "In the POWER TRAIN block the high-side 'Reverse polarity protection' switch T4 is an AON6407 MOSFET whose gate (pin 4) is referenced to GND through D1 (12 V zener) and R19 (33 k), i.e. a gate-to-ground high-side topology. The specification explicitly defines AON6407 as an N-channel MOSFET ('T3 — AON6407 (8-DFN N-MOSFET)') and notes that r1 used a P-channel SI2301 for this exact role, while r2's discrete set does not contain any P-channel device.",
      "expected": "A high-side reverse-polarity switch with the gate tied to ground requires a P-channel MOSFET (source on the output side, body diode oriented input→output, Vgs negative to turn on). An N-channel device in this position cannot turn on (gate at 0 V vs source at +24 V gives Vgs ≈ −24 V, which is both off and beyond the Vgs rating), so the 24 V rail would only pass through the body diode (or not at all) and the gate would be over-stressed.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "POWER TRAIN, sheet 1: 'Reverse polarity protection' block with T4 AON6407, gate via R19 33k and D1 12V to GND, between /+24V_FB (after F2) and +24V"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "T3 — AON6407 (8-DFN N-MOSFET) ... r1 uses ... T1 SI2301 P-MOSFET ... r2 drops to a smaller discrete-switch set (T1/T4 BT3904 ×2, T2 IRLR2905, T3 AON6407, T5 AO3400A ×1)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Where it is silent, judge the design with standard electrical-engineering practice."}
      ],
      "impact": "The 24 V bulk rail is not properly switched on; reverse-polarity protection is ineffective and the MOSFET gate is exposed to a reverse Vgs beyond its rating, likely preventing correct board power-up or damaging the device.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    },
    {
      "category": "extra_component",
      "components": ["PR7"],
      "pins": ["PR7.SDA", "PR7.SCL"],
      "nets": ["NET0086", "NET0069"],
      "observed": "The TF-Luna LiDAR PR7 is drawn wired to the main I2C bus (SDA→NET0086, SCL→NET0069) and to header H7, i.e. it is connected to nets and populated.",
      "expected": "Per the specification the LiDAR is documented but not implemented: 'all four pins (VCC, SDA, SCL, GND) typed free — i.e., it is not connected to any net' and 'on_board: no'.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "WATER LEVEL SENSOR/SWITCHES, sheet 1: PR7 LiDAR pins SDA/SCL tied to NET0086/NET0069 (the ESP32 I2C bus) via H7"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "the r2 netlist shows PR1 with on_board: no ... all four pins ... typed free — i.e., it is not connected to any net in this design."}
      ],
      "impact": "An unmanaged device is placed on the shared I2C bus that firmware never initialises, deviating from the intended build and adding bus capacitance/possible address contention.",
      "confidence": 0.25
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing deviates from the intended design most clearly at the high-side reverse-polarity protection, where an N-channel AON6407 is placed in a gate-to-ground topology that requires a P-channel MOSFET (r1 used SI2301), which would prevent proper 24 V switching and over-stress the gate; a secondary, lower-confidence deviation is that the LiDAR, specified as unwired, is here connected to the main I2C bus."
}

